<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_PACKAGE_TYPE)</string>
    <!-- **Both names, and they are read by different things.** `CFBundleDisplayName`
         is the Home Screen label; `CFBundleName` is what the system quotes in its own
         alerts — including `ASWebAuthenticationSession`'s "… Wants to Use
         accounts.google.com to Sign In", which is the first sentence a new user reads
         and used to quote the build's internal name.
         Not `$(PRODUCT_NAME)`: that is the binary's name and renaming it renames the
         .app, the scheme's product and the Live Activity's host. -->
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>Codename Jo</string>
    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>Codename Jo</string>
    <!-- Names Siri's launcher does not resolve, so an "Open …" phrase reaches the App
         Shortcut instead. A variant of the display name is claimed by the launcher. -->
    <key>INAlternativeAppNames</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>INAlternativeAppName</key>
            <string>Jo</string>
            <key>INAlternativeAppNamePronunciationHint</key>
            <string>Joe</string>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>INAlternativeAppName</key>
            <string>Joe</string>
        </dict>
    </array>
    <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
    <string>1</string>
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
    <key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
    <true/>
    <key>CFBundleIconName</key>
    <string>AppIcon</string>
    <!-- The launch frame is the app ground, not the accent: AccentColor here meant
         every cold start opened on a full-screen brand violet and then cut to a
         near-white (or obsidian) screen. ThemeBackground carries both appearance
         variants, so the launch frame now matches the first real frame in either
         mode. Note this resolves against the *system* appearance — an in-app
         appearance pin can't reach it, since UIUserInterfaceStyle is deliberately
         absent (see below). -->
    <key>UILaunchScreen</key>
    <dict>
        <key>UIColorName</key>
        <string>ThemeBackground</string>
    </dict>
    <key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
    <array>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
    </array>
    <key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
    <false/>
    <!-- `audio` is what makes a background voice session possible at all. Without
         it iOS suspends the process a few seconds after backgrounding, the
         `AVAudioEngine` stops, and the Live Activity's Talk button opens a mic
         gate over a dead graph — silently, which is the worst version of it.

         It is not a keep-alive flag: the process stays alive only while the audio
         session is genuinely running, which is why `RealtimeService` bounds the
         stint with a ten-minute idle timeout and an End button rather than
         leaving it to iOS. Codename Jo both records (the command) and plays (the spoken
         reply) throughout, so this is the mode's intended use.

         There is no `remote-notification`, `fetch` or `processing`: nothing sends
         this app a push, and mail refreshes when the app opens. -->
    <key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
    <array>
        <string>audio</string>
    </array>
    <!-- Not a return of the scheduled refresh above. `BGContinuedProcessingTask` is
         user-started work that keeps running off screen: `HangUpRunway` submits one per
         dispatched job so it can finish after hang-up. The wildcard is because each job
         needs its own identifier — registering one twice kills the app. -->
    <key>BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers</key>
    <array>
        <string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER).dispatch.*</string>
    </array>
    <!-- **The first scene manifest this app has ever had**, added for CarPlay
         (2026-08-17). Before this the app was pure SwiftUI lifecycle — a
         `WindowGroup` plus an `AppDelegate` adaptor — and declaring the car screen
         means declaring the iPhone window alongside it for the first time.

         Three things here are load-bearing and each has a specific failure mode:

         1. **`UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes` must be true.** The phone window
            and the car screen are live at the same time. False makes the CarPlay
            scene request fail rather than degrade.

         2. **The window role carries NO `UISceneDelegateClassName`.** SwiftUI
            installs its own delegate for a `WindowGroup`; naming ours here is how
            the window silently stops being drawn. There is nothing to put there —
            `AppDelegate` is attached with `@UIApplicationDelegateAdaptor` and is
            unaffected by any of this.

         3. **The CarPlay delegate class name is spelled out rather than built from
            `$(PRODUCT_MODULE_NAME)`.** A build-setting substitution that resolves
            empty yields ".CarPlaySceneDelegate", which fails at runtime *on the car
            screen* with nothing wrong on the phone — the most expensive place in
            this app to put a silent failure. The module name is PRODUCT_NAME in
            project.yml; if that is ever renamed, this string is what has to move
            with it, and it is greppable.

         The known trap, recorded so nobody spends a day on it: a SwiftUI-lifecycle
         app declaring a CarPlay scene commonly fails with "Application does not
         implement CarPlay template application lifecycle methods in its scene
         delegate" *even with a correct delegate*, because Xcode's generated scene
         manifest overwrites the hand-written one. Our app target sets
         INFOPLIST_FILE and not GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE (only the three test targets
         generate one), so we are clear of it — do not add INFOPLIST_KEY_* settings
         to the app target, which is how it comes back. -->
    <key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
    <dict>
        <key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
        <true/>
        <key>UISceneConfigurations</key>
        <dict>
            <key>UIWindowSceneSessionRoleApplication</key>
            <array>
                <dict>
                    <key>UISceneConfigurationName</key>
                    <string>Default Configuration</string>
                </dict>
            </array>
            <key>CPTemplateApplicationSceneSessionRoleApplication</key>
            <array>
                <dict>
                    <key>UISceneConfigurationName</key>
                    <string>CarPlay</string>
                    <key>UISceneDelegateClassName</key>
                    <string>CodenameJo.CarPlaySceneDelegate</string>
                </dict>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </dict>
    <!-- Required for ActivityKit. Belongs in the *app's* Info.plist, not the
         widget extension's — the extension declares the WidgetKit extension
         point, the app declares that it may request activities. Omitting it makes
         `Activity.request` throw at runtime with a build that compiles fine. -->
    <key>NSSupportsLiveActivities</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- URL schemes. There must be EXACTLY ONE CFBundleURLTypes key in this
         file — a plist dict with a duplicate key is invalid, and a lenient
         parser keeps only one of them, silently dropping the other scheme.
         Anything that needs a new scheme MUST be appended as another <dict>
         inside the array below, never as a second CFBundleURLTypes key.
         `aurex` carries two things: the Google sign-in callback
         (ASWebAuthenticationSession returns to aurex://google-auth from the
         token server) and the widget-surface deep links, which the
         host distinguishes — aurex://session from the Live Activity,
         aurex://memo from the Home Screen note widget (MemoDeepLink). -->
    <key>JoTokenServerHost</key>
    <string>$(JO_TOKEN_SERVER_HOST)</string>
    <key>JoGitHubClientID</key>
    <string>$(JO_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID)</string>
    <key>JoSlackClientId</key>
    <string>$(JO_SLACK_CLIENT_ID)</string>
    <key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>CFBundleURLName</key>
            <string>$(APP_BUNDLE_ID).oauth</string>
            <key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
            <array>
                <string>aurex</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
    <!-- No UIUserInterfaceStyle: the app follows the system appearance and
         adapts via the Light/Dark color sets in Assets.xcassets. -->

    <key>NSContactsUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Codename Jo looks up a contact's phone number when you ask it to text someone by name.</string>
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Codename Jo uses your microphone to capture voice commands and route them to your connected services.</string>
    <!-- Back as of 2026-08-14, and not for the reason it left. It covered
         SFSpeechRecognizer and went with hold-to-talk; what needs it now is
         SpeechTranscriber, which puts the words on screen without the round trip
         to OpenAI and keeps working while the mic is muted to the call. -->
    <key>NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Codename Jo transcribes what you say on your device so your words appear as you speak them. The transcription never leaves your phone.</string>
    <!-- Required by the linked WebRTC audio library, which references camera
         APIs. Codename Jo is voice-only and does not capture video. Apple requires
         the purpose string to be present regardless (ITMS-90683). -->
    <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Codename Jo does not use the camera. This permission is required by its real-time audio engine and is never used to capture video.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
